        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Elizabeth&#x27;s Spirit-inspired exclamation &#x27;Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb&#x27; (Lk 1:42), Cyril reads the doubled-beatitude (Mary blessed + the Fruit blessed) as the canonical Old-Testament-prophetic-Spirit-utterance preserved in the Lukan canticle structure: Elizabeth is the FIRST New-Testament prophetess, anticipating the broader Pentecost outpouring (Acts 2:18). Tewahedo Marian liturgy cites Lk 1:42 as the canonical anchor of the Ave-Maria pattern preserved in the Wǝddase Maryam and the Salam (Greeting) prayers; the Greeting (sälam) of Mary is the first New-Testament Marian title.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily IV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
