        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>Zacharias&#x27;s tongue is loosed at the moment he writes &#x27;His name is John,&#x27; and Cyril hears in the restoration the canonical sign that speech is given by faith and withheld by unbelief. The priestly silence imposed in the Holy Place is broken when the prophet consents to the angelic message; the canticle Benedictus follows immediately. Tewahedo lectionary preserves Zacharias&#x27;s release as the OT-priestly-to-NT-prophetic hinge of the Lukan infancy.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily III (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
