        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Zacharias&#x27;s Benedictus phrase &#x27;and hath raised up an horn of salvation for us in the house of his servant David&#x27; (Lk 1:69), Cyril reads the horn-of-salvation image as the canonical Davidic-messianic anchor: the horn signifies the strength of royal power, the house of David situates the Messiah in the legitimate covenantal lineage. The Benedictus is the second canonical canticle of Luke (after the Magnificat); Zacharias as priest prophesies the Messiah&#x27;s Davidic descent under the influence of the Spirit. Tewahedo morning-office cites the Benedictus (with the Magnificat and Nunc Dimittis) as the canonical Lukan triad of Spirit-inspired infancy-canticles preserved in the Mäwasǝʾt (Antiphonary).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily V (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
