        21,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s circumcision &#x27;when eight days were accomplished for the circumcising of the child&#x27; (Lk 2:21), Cyril reads the eighth-day circumcision as the canonical Lukan anchor that Christ deliberately submits to the Mosaic law (Gal 4:4 — &#x27;made under the law&#x27;), preserving the Abrahamic covenant-sign in his own flesh. The naming &#x27;Jesus&#x27; at the moment of circumcision links the covenant-sign to the salvation-name. Tewahedo theology — UNIQUELY among major Christian communions still practising bodily circumcision per Jub 15:14-25 — cites Lk 2:21 as the canonical NT anchor for the doctrine that Christ&#x27;s circumcision honors the covenant-sign Christians continue to observe alongside the heart-circumcision (Deu 30:6).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Gal 4:4; Jub 15:14-25 (R.H. Charles trans., 1902 — PD).",
