        35,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Simeon&#x27;s prophecy to Mary &#x27;yea, a sword shall pierce through thy own soul also&#x27; (Lk 2:35), Cyril reads the sword-image as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Marian compassion-at-the-Cross — preserved more fully in Jn 19:25 (&#x27;there stood by the cross of Jesus his mother&#x27;). The sword is not Mary&#x27;s physical death but the maternal-soul-piercing of watching her Son crucified. Tewahedo Marian piety cites Lk 2:35 as the canonical Old-Testament-prophetic anchor that Mary&#x27;s joys are inseparable from her sorrows; the seven-swords iconography preserved in the Tewahedo Wǝddase Maryam draws together the doxology of the Virgin with her maternal-passion compassion.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
