    (
        2,
        52,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and man&#x27; (Lk 2:52), Cyril reads the fourfold growth-formula (wisdom + stature + favour-with-God + favour-with-man) as the canonical Lukan anti-Apollinarian anchor: the Word does not pretend to grow but truly progresses in the integrated dimensions of his assumed humanity. Cyril&#x27;s exegesis preserves both the eternal completeness of the Word and the genuine developmental finitude of the assumed nature without confusion. Tewahedo Miaphysite Christology cites Lk 2:52 as the canonical doubled-witness with Lk 2:40 against any Christology that denies Christ&#x27;s true human soul-growth.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XIV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
    (
        2,
        52,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos III.51</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and man&#x27; — Athanasius marks proekopten sophia kai hēlikia (he advanced in wisdom and stature) as a QUA-FLESH developmental-increase, NOT a qua-Word increase-in-wisdom (the Word is the Father&#x27;s-Wisdom by-essence and cannot become-wiser). The advancement names the assumed-flesh&#x27;s-real-growth: an infant-Word grows into a young-Word; a young-Word learns the carpenter&#x27;s-trade; the developmentally-real human-experience is genuinely-experienced by the Word-incarnate without compromising the Word&#x27;s-eternal-perfection-in-knowledge. Crucial anti-Docetic and anti-Apollinarian text: Christ&#x27;s-humanity is developmentally-genuine, not pre-formed-or-shortcut. Tewahedo Christology preserves this developmental-realism in its catechesis of the child-Jesus&#x27;s growth.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
    ),
