    (
        4,
        1,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;Jesus being full of the Holy Ghost returned from Jordan, and was led by the Spirit into the wilderness&#x27; (Lk 4:1), Cyril reads the doubled-pneumatology (full-of-Spirit + led-by-Spirit) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the Spirit-anointing at the Jordan flows immediately into the public ministry&#x27;s testing: there is no gap between empowerment and trial. Cyril preserves the parallel pattern in apostolic-Christian life: Spirit-baptism (sacramental Confirmation) precedes-but-does-not-prevent the wilderness of testing. Tewahedo monastic-formation cites Lk 4:1 as the canonical anchor that the Spirit who fills also leads-into-wilderness; the bahǝtawi tradition is the appropriation of this Lukan pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
