        43,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent&#x27; (Lk 4:43), Cyril reads the dei (it-is-necessary) + apestalmai (I-am-sent) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Christ&#x27;s missional self-understanding: the Word&#x27;s earthly ministry is mission-bound from above, not crowd-driven from below. The verb apostellō becomes the New-Testament technical-term for divine-sending; Christ&#x27;s apostolic-mission is the model for the apostolic-mission of the disciples (Jn 20:21 — &#x27;as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you&#x27;). Tewahedo missionary theology cites Lk 4:43 + Jn 20:21 as the canonical doubled-witness that the Tewahedo evangelistic mission to the Beta-Israel and surrounding nations participates in Christ&#x27;s own apostolic-sending.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXXIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 20:21.",
