        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;But that ye may know that the Son of man hath power upon earth to forgive sins&#x27; (Lk 5:24), Cyril reads the title &#x27;Son of Man&#x27; coupled with divine-prerogative-to-forgive as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Christological argument that runs through the Synoptics: only God forgives sins (so the scribes correctly observe at Mk 2:7), the Son of Man forgives sins, therefore the Son of Man is divine. The miracle of healing the paralytic is the sign confirming the antecedent claim. Tewahedo absolution-theology cites Lk 5:24 as the canonical anchor for the priestly absolution formula — the priest forgives in persona Christi, exercising the authority Christ gave the apostles (Jn 20:23).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXXVIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mk 2:7, Jn 20:23.",
