        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>The Lukan Beatitudes — &#x27;Blessed are ye poor, for yours is the kingdom of God&#x27; — are for Cyril the canonical pendant to the Matthean form: where Matthew preserves the inner-disposition reading (poor in spirit), Luke preserves the outer-condition reading (literally poor). Cyril harmonizes the two as a single canonical blessing of those whose poverty — whether of possession or of spirit — opens them to the kingdom. Tewahedo monastic catechesis draws explicitly on the Lukan form.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
