        27,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Love your enemies, do good to them which hate you&#x27; (Lk 6:27), Cyril reads the love-your-enemies command as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that Christian ethics transcends Old-Testament-reciprocity: the love-of-enemies is impossible without participation in the Father&#x27;s own love (Mt 5:45 — &#x27;he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good&#x27;). The command is not natural-virtue but supernatural-grace. Tewahedo monastic ethics cites Lk 6:27 + Mt 5:44-45 as the canonical doubled-witness for the bahǝtawi&#x27;s practice of love-of-enemies — the desert prayer &#x27;forgive my enemies as Thou hast forgiven me&#x27; is the appropriation of this command.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XLIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 5:44-45.",
