    (
        7,
        12,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the raising of the Nain widow&#x27;s son — &#x27;and there came a fear on all: and they glorified God, saying, That a great prophet is risen up among us&#x27; (Lk 7:12, 16) — Cyril reads the first resurrection-miracle in Luke as the canonical Lukan anchor of Christ&#x27;s master-over-death authority, prefiguring the Lazarus raising (Jn 11) and ultimately Christ&#x27;s own resurrection. The Nain widow&#x27;s son is the third raising-from-the-dead in scripture (after Elijah at Zarephath and Elisha at Shunem); Christ&#x27;s act surpasses the prophets — he raises without prayer, by his own word. Tewahedo funeral office cites Lk 7:12 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that Christ&#x27;s compassion at the bier of the widow&#x27;s son is the model for priestly compassion at every funeral.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XLVII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
