    (
        9,
        62,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;No man, having put his hand to the plough, and looking back, is fit for the kingdom of God&#x27; (Lk 9:62), Cyril reads the plough-image as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the kingdom requires unbroken forward-orientation: the disciple resembles Elisha (1 Ki 19:19-21) who left the plough to follow Elijah without looking back. The image of the plough turning the soil prefigures the apostolic mission turning the soil of the nations for the seed of the gospel. Tewahedo monastic-formation cites Lk 9:62 + 1 Ki 19:19-21 as the canonical doubled-witness for the irreversibility of monastic profession: once the bahǝtawi has begun, he does not look back.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. 1 Ki 19:19-21.",
    ),
