    (
        10,
        18,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven&#x27; (Lk 10:18), Cyril reads the seventy-two&#x27;s return-with-joy + Christ&#x27;s prophetic-vision as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the apostolic-missionary expansion enacts in time the eternally-decided defeat of Satan. The lightning-image draws on Is 14:12 (Lucifer fall) and is parallel to Rev 12:7-9. Tewahedo angelology, drawing on 1 Enoch 86 (Mäṣḥafä Hēnok&#x27;s account of fallen stars), cites Lk 10:18 + 1 En 86 + Rev 12:7-9 as the canonical triple-witness of Satan&#x27;s heavenly-fall — the same fall is the eschatological referent of every successful exorcism in apostolic mission.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Is 14:12, 1 En 86 (R.H. Charles, 1912 — PD), Rev 12:7-9.",
    ),
