        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos III.46</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;No man knoweth who the Son is, but the Father; and who the Father is, but the Son, and he to whom the Son will reveal him&#x27; — Athanasius marks Lk 10:22 as the LUKAN-PARALLEL of Mt 11:27 (treated already in γ.4.9 seed) — the MUTUAL-KNOWLEDGE-FORMULA as homoousion-prooftext. The Lukan-form&#x27;s variation (oudeis ginōskei tis estin ho huios ei mē ho Patēr — &#x27;no one knoweth WHO the Son is&#x27;) emphasizes the WHO-question: the Son&#x27;s-identity is known exhaustively-only-by-the-Father, and reciprocally. Only-equals-by-nature exhaustively-know-each-other (a creature could not exhaustively-know the Father). The exhaustive-mutual-knowledge therefore-requires homoousion. Pairs with Mt 11:27 as the canonical-synoptic mutual-knowledge-homoousion pair.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
