        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>Cyril reads the Good Samaritan parable as a Christological allegory from at least the third Christian century, and his exegesis carries the canonical pattern: the wounded man is Adam-fallen-among-thieves; the priest and Levite are Law and Prophets, unable to save; the Samaritan is the despised-and-rejected Christ; the inn is the Church; the two denarii are the Old and New Testaments; the promise to return is the Second Coming. Tewahedo monastic exegesis preserves this allegory wholesale.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXVIII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
