        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Lukan Lord&#x27;s-Prayer petition &#x27;And forgive us our sins; for we also forgive every one that is indebted to us&#x27; (Lk 11:4), Cyril reads the forgive-as-forgiven-by-us clause as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that human forgiveness of debtors is the operative condition (not the meritorious cause) of receiving divine forgiveness. The clause&#x27;s reciprocal grammar makes the Lord&#x27;s Prayer the only canonical petitionary text that requires the petitioner to take an ethical action FIRST. Tewahedo penance-theology cites Lk 11:4 + Mt 6:12 + Mt 6:14-15 as the canonical triple-witness that priestly absolution presupposes the penitent&#x27;s forgiveness of debtors; sacramental confession is incomplete without horizontal reconciliation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXVII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 6:12, Mt 6:14-15.",
