        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;how much more shall your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him&#x27; (Lk 11:13), Cyril reads the distinctive-Lukan substitution of &#x27;Holy Spirit&#x27; (where Matthew has &#x27;good things&#x27; at Mt 7:11) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the supreme petition of Christian prayer is the gift of the Spirit himself, not merely material or providential goods. Luke&#x27;s pneumatological theology is integrated throughout: the Spirit is the Promise-of-the-Father (Lk 24:49 + Acts 1:4-5) and the gift to which all other gifts are subordinate. Tewahedo charismatic-prayer-theology cites Lk 11:13 as the canonical Lukan anchor for the doctrine that the Holy Spirit is the answer to every prayer &#x27;in Jesus&#x27; name&#x27;.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 7:11, Lk 24:49, Acts 1:4-5.",
