    (
        11,
        27,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the unnamed woman&#x27;s exclamation &#x27;Blessed is the womb that bare thee, and the paps which thou hast sucked&#x27; (Lk 11:27), Cyril reads the public Marian beatitude — uniquely Lukan among the Synoptics — as the canonical Lukan-public-anchor of the Theotokos doctrine: even mid-public-ministry, a Galilean crowd-woman utters spontaneous Marian-praise. Christ&#x27;s response &#x27;yea rather, blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it&#x27; does NOT cancel her praise but transfers its principle: Mary&#x27;s blessedness is not biological-only but also the FIRST exemplar of hearing-and-keeping (Lk 1:38). Tewahedo Wǝddase Maryam preserves Lk 11:27 alongside Lk 1:42 as the canonical doubled-Lukan public-Marian-beatitudes.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXXII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
