        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s lament &#x27;O Jerusalem, Jerusalem... how often would I have gathered thy children together, as a hen doth gather her brood under her wings, and ye would not!&#x27; (Lk 13:34), Cyril reads the doubly-vocative city-address + maternal-hen-image as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Christ&#x27;s divine-anguish over human refusal: the Word is Maker-Mother of Israel — the &#x27;how often&#x27; acknowledges the prophetic outreach to Jerusalem across centuries (Christ identifies himself as the agent of that outreach). The maternal hen-and-chicks image preserves the Old-Testament eagle-wings imagery (Exo 19:4, Deu 32:11) in maternal-form. Tewahedo Marian theology cites Lk 13:34 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the maternal-Christ-image alongside Mary&#x27;s own maternal-Theotokos role.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XCVIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Exo 19:4, Deu 32:11.",
