        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the master&#x27;s command &#x27;Go out into the highways and hedges, and compel them to come in&#x27; (Lk 14:23), Cyril reads the compel-them-to-come-in (anagkason eiselthein) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of evangelistic-persuasive-urgency: the apostolic invitation is not coy permission but earnest-pressing constraint. Cyril is emphatic that the constraint is moral-persuasive, never physical-coercive (Augustine&#x27;s later use of this verse to justify coercion against the Donatists is not Cyril&#x27;s reading). Tewahedo missionary-pastoral theology cites Lk 14:23 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that evangelistic urgency is genuine pressure of love, not state-religious coercion.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CIV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
