        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple&#x27; (Lk 14:33), Cyril reads the forsake-all-that-he-hath (panta apotassetai tois huparchousin) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of total-discipleship: the cost-of-discipleship parable (counting-the-tower + counting-the-battle) issues in this absolute. Cyril does not read &#x27;forsake&#x27; as universal-divestment for every Christian but as the inner-detachment that some are called to embody outwardly in monastic profession. Tewahedo monastic theology cites Lk 14:33 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the bahǝtawi&#x27;s vow of poverty — the monastic outwardly enacts what every disciple inwardly possesses.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
