        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the parable of the lost coin — &#x27;Either what woman having ten pieces of silver, if she lose one piece, doth not light a candle, and sweep the house, and seek diligently till she find it?&#x27; (Lk 15:8) — Cyril reads the maternal-housekeeping-image as the canonical Lukan anchor of a doctrine that complements the shepherd-image (Lk 15:4) and the father-image (Lk 15:11): God seeks the lost in three iconic ways — shepherd seeking on the hills, woman seeking in the house, father waiting at the door. The triple-image is Trinitarian-iconic-pedagogy. Tewahedo Trinitarian-iconography cites Lk 15:4-32 as the canonical Lukan iconographic triple-pattern of the Father-Son-Spirit&#x27;s-shared-search-for-the-lost.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CVIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
