        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the father&#x27;s final words to the elder brother &#x27;It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad: for this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found&#x27; (Lk 15:32), Cyril reads the doubled-formula (dead-and-alive + lost-and-found) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of conversion-as-resurrection: the prodigal&#x27;s return is not merely moral-improvement but a passage from death to life. The same doubled-formula echoes Christian baptism (Rom 6:4 — dead with Christ, alive in Christ). Tewahedo baptismal-and-penance theology cites Lk 15:32 + Rom 6:4 as the canonical doubled-witness that every conversion is a participation in the death-and-resurrection of Christ; the Tewahedo penance-rite preserves the alive-again grammar in its absolution formula.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Rom 6:4.",
