        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the master&#x27;s commendation of the unjust steward — &#x27;the lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely&#x27; (Lk 16:8) — Cyril reads the lord-commended-the-unjust paradox as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that prudent-shrewdness in temporal matters should be matched by prudent-shrewdness in eternal matters: the steward is praised not for his dishonesty but for his foresight in securing his future. Tewahedo stewardship theology cites Lk 16:8 + Lk 16:9 (&#x27;make to yourselves friends of the mammon of unrighteousness&#x27;) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine of strategic-almsgiving — the prudent Christian uses temporal wealth to build eternal friendships (the recipients of the alms welcome the giver into eternal habitations).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
