        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>Cyril reads the Rich-Man-and-Lazarus narrative as canonically settling several intermediate-state questions: that there is consciousness after death; that the just are gathered to a place of refreshment (&#x27;Abraham&#x27;s bosom&#x27;); that the unjust suffer torment in conscious recognition; that the chasm fixed between the two is uncrossable; that Moses and the Prophets are sufficient witnesses to deter the living. Tewahedo eschatology draws on Lk 16:19-31 as the principal canonical Lukan anchor of the intermediate-state doctrine.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXI (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
