        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>Only the Samaritan among the ten cleansed lepers returns to give thanks at Christ&#x27;s feet, and Cyril reads the verse as the canonical Lukan anchor of the eucharistic posture: the cleansed turn back to the cleanser in thanksgiving (eucharistia). The nine who do not return are not cured of the deeper leprosy of ingratitude. Tewahedo Anaphora preserves the Cyrillian reading: the proper response to divine cleansing is the return-to-the-altar with thanks.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXIII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
