        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the parable of the wicked husbandmen (Lk 20:9-16), Cyril reads the vineyard-tenants who reject the servants and finally kill the heir as the canonical Lukan anchor of the salvation-history-as-progressive-rejection: prophets sent, prophets killed; finally the Son sent, the Son killed; the vineyard given to others (Gentiles + Tewahedo). The &#x27;heir&#x27; language is Christological: Christ&#x27;s death is the prophetic-final rejection that opens the vineyard to the wider mission. Tewahedo missionary-theology cites Lk 20:9-16 + Mt 21:43 (&#x27;the kingdom of God shall be taken from you, and given to a nation bringing forth the fruits thereof&#x27;) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine that the Gentile-Ethiopian inclusion in the vineyard is the consequence of the canonical-prophetic rejection of Christ at Calvary.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXXII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 21:43.",
