        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Render therefore unto Caesar the things which be Caesar&#x27;s, and unto God the things which be God&#x27;s&#x27; — Cyril treats the saying as the canonical two-spheres principle: civic duty is real but subordinate, and never displaces the duty to God. The denarius carries Caesar&#x27;s image; the soul carries God&#x27;s. The implicit argument is that the soul is rendered to God by being conformed to his image. Tewahedo political theology cites Lk 20:25 as the canonical two-kingdoms anchor.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXXII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
