        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Sadducees&#x27; resurrection-denying scenario (the woman-with-seven-husbands at Lk 20:27-33), Cyril reads the Sadducees&#x27; rejection-of-resurrection as the canonical anti-pattern: those who deny the resurrection prove they neither know the Scriptures (the basis of resurrection-hope) nor the power of God (the agent of resurrection). The Sadducees&#x27; literalistic-marriage scenario fundamentally misunderstands that resurrection-life is transformed-life, not extended-marriage. Tewahedo eschatology cites Lk 20:27-40 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the resurrection is the trans-marital + trans-procreative life of the Kingdom, distinct from earthly marriage&#x27;s covenantal exclusivity.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXXIV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
