        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s puzzle from Psalm 110 — &#x27;David himself saith in the book of Psalms, The LORD said unto my Lord, Sit thou on my right hand&#x27; (Lk 20:42, citing Ps 110:1) — Cyril reads the Psalm-110 citation as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Christological double-Lordship: David (the messiah&#x27;s ancestor) addresses the messiah AS HIS LORD, which makes the messiah more than merely David&#x27;s son. Ps 110:1 becomes the most-cited Old-Testament verse in the New Testament (~25 times) and the foundational text for Christ&#x27;s session at the Father&#x27;s right hand (Ascension theology). Tewahedo Christology cites Lk 20:42 + Ps 110:1 as the canonical Old-Testament-Christological double-Lordship anchor; the Tewahedo Anaphora preserves the right-hand-of-the-Father language verbatim.</p></aside>',
