        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s promise &#x27;I will give you a mouth and wisdom, which all your adversaries shall not be able to gainsay nor resist&#x27; (Lk 21:15), Cyril reads the doubled-gift (mouth-and-wisdom) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the Spirit&#x27;s-utterance-in-persecution: when the disciple is delivered up before kings, the Spirit speaks IN him. The doctrine flows into Stephen&#x27;s defense before the Sanhedrin (Acts 6:10 — &#x27;they were not able to resist the wisdom and the spirit by which he spake&#x27;). Tewahedo confessor-theology cites Lk 21:15 + Acts 6:10 as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine of the Spirit-given confessor&#x27;s-utterance; the Tewahedo martyrs (e.g. Ethiopian Christians under Imperial persecution) participate in this canonical promise.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Acts 6:10.",
