        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;But there shall not an hair of your head perish&#x27; (Lk 21:18), Cyril reads the hyperbolic-hair-promise in juxtaposition with the immediately-preceding &#x27;some of you shall they cause to be put to death&#x27; (21:16) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the spiritual-preservation-through-bodily-death: the believer who dies for Christ does not perish (the hair-of-the-head is not lost) — the apparent perishing is preserved-life in the heavenly economy. Tewahedo martyr-theology cites Lk 21:18 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of resurrection-preservation: every hair of the Tewahedo martyrs (Saint Yared, the Nine Saints, Imperial-era confessors) is preserved with God.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
