        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s Gethsemane prayer &#x27;Father, if thou be willing, remove this cup from me: nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done&#x27; (Lk 22:42), Cyril reads the not-my-will-but-thine as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Christ&#x27;s two-wills in the unity of the one-person: the human-will of Christ truly desires deliverance, the divine-will (shared with the Father) wills the salvific-Passion. The hypostatic-union does not absorb the human will but ratifies its perfect-obedient-subordination to the divine. Tewahedo Christology — Miaphysite-with-two-operations — cites Lk 22:42 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that Christ in his humanity prays the prayer the entire human race must learn to pray: &#x27;not my will but thine&#x27;.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
