        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Lukan-unique addition &#x27;And there appeared an angel unto him from heaven, strengthening him&#x27; (Lk 22:43), Cyril reads the Gethsemane-angel-strengthening as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of angelic-ministry to Christ in his true-human extremity: the same angels that ministered to him after the Temptation (Mt 4:11) now minister at the Passion&#x27;s edge. The Word made flesh receives angelic strengthening — not because the divinity needs it, but because the humanity legitimately does. Tewahedo angelology cites Lk 22:43 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of angelic-ministry to suffering Christians; the Tewahedo Angelic-Intercession tradition (the &#x27;Mäshafä Mäla&#x27;ǝkt&#x27; supplications) draws on this canonical pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 4:11.",
