        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s word to the arresting-officers &#x27;this is your hour, and the power of darkness&#x27; (Lk 22:53), Cyril reads the doubled-phrase (your-hour + power-of-darkness) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Satan-permitted-darkness-hour: the Passion proceeds not because Christ is defeated but because the Father permits the hour of the powers-of-darkness. The exousia tou skotous (authority of darkness) is the same authority Christ acknowledges at his arrest as permitted but not victorious. Tewahedo Holy-Week theology cites Lk 22:53 + Col 1:13 (delivered from the power of darkness) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine that the Cross goes through the darkness-hour to the resurrection-light.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLVII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Col 1:13.",
