        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Jerusalem-mob&#x27;s cry &#x27;Crucify him, crucify him&#x27; (Lk 23:21), Cyril reads the doubled-imperative as the canonical Lukan anchor of the canonical-prophetic-rejection&#x27;s voice: the cry that reverses the Hosanna of Lk 19:38 by the same population. Cyril is careful to distinguish the city-political mob from &#x27;the Jewish people&#x27; as a whole (against any later anti-Judaic reading); the cry is the act of a Jerusalem-Caiaphas-orchestrated faction. Tewahedo pastoral theology cites Lk 23:21 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the same human voice can ascribe Hosanna-and-Crucify within a single week — the warning against fickle-religiosity.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
