        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s word to the Lukan-distinctive &#x27;daughters of Jerusalem&#x27; — &#x27;Weep not for me, but weep for yourselves, and for your children&#x27; (Lk 23:28) — Cyril reads the redirected-tears as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of properly-ordered-sorrow: the Christian does not pity the Crucified (he chose the Cross) but laments his/her own sin (whose burden he bears). The prophetic-Tewahedo lamentation tradition (the bēbet-äbiyot procession of Holy Week) preserves the redirected-sorrow pattern: the weeping is for our sins, not Christ&#x27;s death. Tewahedo Holy-Week theology cites Lk 23:28 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of redirected-Holy-Week-lament.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CL (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
