        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the cross-superscription &#x27;THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS&#x27; (Lk 23:38 — in Greek, Latin, Hebrew per Jn 19:20), Cyril reads the trilingual-titulus as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the universal-kingship-of-Christ proclaimed at the Cross: Pilate&#x27;s intended-mockery becomes (against his intent) the canonical proclamation of Christ&#x27;s kingship in the three languages of the known world (Hebrew = covenantal, Greek = cultural, Latin = imperial). Tewahedo Solomonic-dynasty self-understanding cites Lk 23:38 + Jn 19:20 as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine that the King of the Jews is also the King of all nations — the Ethiopian Solomonic-dynastic claim to Christ-the-King derives from this universal-trilingual-titulus.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 19:20.",
