        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the cosmic-darkness from the sixth to the ninth hour (Lk 23:44), Cyril reads the three-hour-darkness as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the cosmic-participation in the Passion: creation darkens because its Maker dies. The three hours echo Jonah&#x27;s three days in the fish (Mt 12:40), the three days Moses was in the ark (Jub 47:5), and the three days Christ will be in the tomb. Tewahedo cosmic-Christology cites Lk 23:44 + Mt 27:51-52 (earth-quake, rocks rent, graves opened) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine that the natural-cosmic-order participates in the death of its Word-Maker; the Tewahedo Holy-Friday liturgy preserves the three-hour darkening observance.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 27:51-52.",
