        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Temple-veil rent in twain at Christ&#x27;s death (Lk 23:45), Cyril reads the schism-of-the-veil as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the opening-of-access to the Holy-of-Holies through Christ&#x27;s flesh (Heb 10:19-20 — &#x27;a new and living way, which he hath consecrated for us, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh&#x27;). The veil rent from top to bottom (Mt 27:51) signals God&#x27;s-direction rather than human-direction of the action. Tewahedo eucharistic theology cites Lk 23:45 + Heb 10:19-20 as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine that the iconographic-curtain in the Tewahedo sanctuary (which veils the maqdas) functions as both barrier-and-passage — the curtain that screens is also the curtain that opens at the Eucharistic moment.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Heb 10:19-20.",
