        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Joseph of Arimathea&#x27;s wrapping of Christ&#x27;s body and burial &#x27;in a sepulchre that was hewn in stone, wherein never man before was laid&#x27; (Lk 23:53), Cyril reads the new-unused-tomb as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the sanctified-tomb: just as the virgin-womb prepared the Word&#x27;s entry into the world, the new-tomb (Joseph&#x27;s own, never used) prepares his three-day rest. The pairing virgin-womb + new-tomb forms the canonical-bookend of the Incarnation. Tewahedo Marian-and-Easter theology cites Lk 23:53 + Lk 1:34-35 (&#x27;how shall this be, seeing I know not a man&#x27;) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine of the sanctified-virginal-spaces that bound the Incarnation.</p></aside>',
