        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Emmaus-disciples&#x27; retrospective &#x27;Did not our heart burn within us, while he talked with us by the way, and while he opened to us the scriptures?&#x27; (Lk 24:32), Cyril reads the burning-heart formula as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the affective-pneumatological response to Scripture-rightly-expounded: the heart-burn is the Spirit&#x27;s-confirmation of the Christological exegesis. The same heart-burn recurs at Pentecost (Acts 2:3 — &#x27;cloven tongues like as of fire&#x27;). Tewahedo monastic-mystical theology cites Lk 24:32 + Acts 2:3 as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine of the Spirit-given heart-burn at canonical-Scripture-encounter; the Tewahedo daily Mass preserves the Word-and-Sacrament heart-burning pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Acts 2:3.",
