        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the risen Christ&#x27;s final commission &#x27;behold, I send the promise of my Father upon you: but tarry ye in the city of Jerusalem, until ye be endued with power from on high&#x27; (Lk 24:49), Cyril reads the doubled-promise (promise-of-the-Father + power-from-on-high) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Pentecost-anticipation: the Promise-of-the-Father is the Holy Spirit (cf. Acts 1:4-5, Acts 2:33), the power-from-on-high is the Spirit&#x27;s-coming-down. The verse is the bridge from the Gospel of Luke to Acts; the same Spirit who descended on Christ at the Jordan (Lk 3:22) will descend on the Church at Pentecost. Tewahedo pneumatology cites Lk 24:49 + Acts 1:4-5 + Acts 2:1-4 as the canonical Lukan-Acts triple-witness for the Pentecostal Promise; the Tewahedo Pentecost feast (Pärräqlēṭos) preserves this canonical Lukan promise-fulfilment.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Acts 1:4-5, Acts 2:1-4. Tewahedo liturgical witness: Pärräqlēṭos (Pentecost).",
